 failed tcpdump attempt left /tmp/dhcp.pcap.log (cmd-84cd5960e6484bae). None of these files were cleaned up: the after_executor temporary-files snapshot for node2 still lists /tmp/dhcp.pcap.log, /tmp/dhcpsniff.py, /tmp/sniff.log, and /tmp/sniff.pid as residue. This is bounded, low-risk, non-security-relevant leftover (does not affect DHCP functionality or expose anything), but it is abandoned troubleshooting residue that was never restored.","evidence_ids":["cmd-c36530c8bd9a4669","cmd-5e2c86d217244fdd","cmd-1fe245b805164804","cmd-5f94920cd61549b5","cmd-33761ab306b34c39","cmd-d109a13399e04987","cmd-f85a9051f2614b21","cmd-75cb7b7594e44151","cmd-84cd5960e6484bae","global:after_executor:node2:temporary-files"]},"overall_summary":"All three material outcomes of the task were satisfied with direct, coherent evidence including a full restart transition (pre-restart baseline, restart acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and post-restart recovery) for every node. node1 runs a Kea DHCPv4 server on the shared 10.100.56.0/24 network with reservations for node2 and node3; node2 and node3 were reconfigured (via a dhclient 'reject' directive, since both nodes were already using platform DHCP rather than static addressing) to deterministically obtain their leases from node1, and this was verified across ifdown/ifup renewal, a full reboot cycle, and cross-checked against node1's Kea lease database. Hygiene is moderate: two troubleshooting mutations (firewalld/iptables DROP rules) were cleanly reverted, but a custom DHCP-sniffing script and its output/log/pid files, plus a failed tcpdump log, were left behind unremoved on node2.","limitations":["The task instruction assumed node2/node3 started with a static IP assignment, but the captured baseline shows they were already using dynamic DHCP from the platform gateway (10.100.56.1); the executor's solution (rejecting the platform's offers to source leases from node1 instead) satisfies the evident intent but is not a literal 'static-to-DHCP' conversion.","Package-health checks for node2 and node3 were unavailable (timeout) in both before_prepare and after_executor snapshots, so package-manager consistency could not be independently verified before or after the task.","No evidence was captured showing node2/node3 DHCP behavior with the platform gateway's DHCP server fully disabled or absent; reliance on client-side rejection of the platform's offers/NACKs is a workaround rather than eliminating the competing DHCP source, though this appears to be an environment constraint outside the executor's control."]},"ttft_ms":45035,"type":"result","duration_ms":90426,"uuid":"0cb685b8-4bcb-4a14-a270-0a2edd0c5cdd","ttft_stream_ms":1912,"time_to_request_ms":48,"queued_turn_count":0}
